      "title": "[Adafruit Blog] A mountain of AI math, a few precious bytes",
      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=blog.adafruit.com&amp;sz=128\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 0.75em;padding:5px 10px;background:#f0f4f8;border-left:3px solid #7b9fc4;border-radius:3px;font-size:0.82em;color:#444;line-height:1.8;\">🔧 <a href=\"https://zirnhelt.github.io/super-rss-feed/review.html?url=https%3A%2F%2Fblog.adafruit.com%2F2026%2F10%2F10%2Fa-mountain-of-ai-math-a-few-precious-bytes%2F\" style=\"color:#7b9fc4;text-decoration:none;\">✏️</a></p>\nCo-authored by Phillip Torrone and Mikey Sklar. Making CircuitPython fit is a recurring job. Firmware grows, a board gets close to its flash limit or goes over, and maintainers find room through compression, code sharing and other tricks. One Trinket M0 build was eight bytes over. That’s one example of a cycle the team has […]",
      "date_published": "2026-10-10T21:52:37+00:00",
