      "content_html": "<img src=\"https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/embedded_3d_printing_prusa_silicone_hair_gel_featured_rileys_lab_youtube.jpg\" style=\"width:100%;max-height:300px;object-fit:cover;\"/>\n<p style=\"margin:0 0 0.75em;padding:5px 10px;background:#f0f4f8;border-left:3px solid #7b9fc4;border-radius:3px;font-size:0.82em;color:#444;line-height:1.8;\">🎨 🌾 🔧 🪶 🌲 🏔️ 🔭 <a href=\"https://zirnhelt.github.io/super-rss-feed/review.html?url=https%3A%2F%2Fhackaday.com%2F2026%2F10%2F09%2Fembedded-3d-printing-with-liquids-on-a-bricked-prusa-fdm-printer%2F\" style=\"color:#7b9fc4;text-decoration:none;\">✏️</a></p>\n<div><img alt=\"\" class=\"attachment-large size-large wp-post-image\" height=\"466\" src=\"https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/embedded_3d_printing_prusa_silicone_hair_gel_featured_rileys_lab_youtube.jpg?w=800\" style=\"margin:0 auto;margin-bottom:15px;\" width=\"800\"/></div>Recently [Riley] got his paws on an old Prusa i3 FDM printer that had clearly seen better days, with a hot-end that had suffered an explosive blow-out. For the project <a class=\"read-more\" href=\"https://hackaday.com/2026/10/09/embedded-3d-printing-with-liquids-on-a-bricked-prusa-fdm-printer/\">…read more</a>",
